#### TPC Acceptance & Track Quality Cut Efficiency

The track Reconstruction (Rc) efficiency and TPC acceptance have been obtained by embedding Monte-Carlo (MC) \(\phi\to e^{+}e^{-}\) tracks into real events at the detector's response level with GEANT and reconstructing them via the STAR production chain along with real tracks. In this way, the efficiency includes the TPC geometry acceptance and the track reconstruction efficiency.

While the desired final result is \(dN/dy\) in \(|y|\leq 1\), in the real measurement \(dN/dy|_{|y|\leq 1}\) might or might not include the \(\phi\) signals from outside of the \(y\) range due to the detector resolution and the limited detector acceptance, \(|\eta|\leq 1\). To correct for these effects, the MC parent particles, \(\phi\), are generated for not only \(|y|\leq 1\) but also beyond the \(|y|=1\) limits. Because the detector

Figure 4.19: PID efficiency and contamination yieldsresolution in \(\eta\) or \(y\) is only a few percent, generating the MC parents within \(|y|\leq 1.2\) was sufficient.

We also estimate the track quality cuts efficiency by applying the track quality cut used in our analysis to the reconstructed tracks. At this point, the TOF matching efficiency and PID efficiency are applied to each daughter track. If both daughters, \(e^{+}e^{-}\), are reconstructed after all of the efficiencies are applied, the parent \(\phi\) is counted as reconstructed. The final pair efficiency is calculated as

\[\text{Eff}^{total}\equiv N_{Rc}^{\phi\to e^{+}e^{-}}/N_{MC}^{\phi\to e^{+}e^{-}}. \tag{4.14}\]

In order to obtain correct efficiency for \(dN/dy|_{|y|\leq 1}\), the MC \(\phi\)s are counted only within \(|y|\leq 1\) and the Rc \(\phi\)s are counted within \(|y|\leq 1\) regardless if the input (MC) \(\phi\) originates at a \(|y|\) larger than 1 to simulate real measurements. The errors on the efficiency are calculated with the Bayesian approach [46], as follows,

\[Err\equiv\sqrt{\frac{N_{Rc}+1}{N_{MC}+2}\left(\frac{N_{Rc}+2}{N_{MC}+3}-\frac {N_{Rc}+1}{N_{MC}+2}\right)}. \tag{4.15}\]

Figure 4.20 shows the changes of \(\phi\) reconstruction efficiency as each efficiency is applied. The total \(\phi\) reconstruction efficiency is presented in purple at the bottom of the figure.

